= BITwiki organization =

BITwiki is organized so knowledge can be:

* read as coherent pages;
* decomposed into reusable Book Matter where useful;
* queried as semantic data;
* traversed through explicit relationships;
* entered through portals, categories, indices, outlines, and search;
* audited through sources, provenance, uncertainty, and revision history;
* preserved without confusing the current filesystem/navigation surface with the ontology.

== The architecture in one view ==

<pre>
                         KNOWLEDGE OBJECT
                               │
       ┌───────────────────────┼────────────────────────┐
       │                       │                        │
    IDENTITY                CONTENT                 EPISTEMICS
       │                       │                        │
 Entity type               Book Matter              Evidence
 Domain(s)                 Overview                 Sources
 Relationships             Theory / model           Provenance
 Scope                     Mechanism                Standing
                            Methods                  Uncertainty
                            Evidence                 Review
                            Applications             Lifecycle
                            References
       │                       │                        │
       └───────────────────────┼────────────────────────┘
                               │
                         NAVIGATION / VIEWS
                  Portal · Category · Index · Query
</pre>

No branch in this diagram is the whole system.

== MediaWiki primitives ==

=== Page ===
A durable knowledge object or project/documentation unit.

=== Namespace ===
A broad system/page-class boundary with MediaWiki behavior. Namespace is not subject taxonomy.

=== Category ===
A human-readable browse/index surface. Category hierarchy is a navigation graph, not the ontology.

=== Template ===
Reusable/transcluded structure, interface, or content pattern.

=== Property ===
Semantic MediaWiki attribute or relationship with an explicitly documented meaning.

=== Concept ===
A stored semantic query/computed collection when that capability provides value. It is not synonymous with V2 entity type <code>Concept</code>.

=== Module ===
Reusable executable wiki logic when templates are insufficient.

=== Redirect ===
Title continuity and migration mechanism. Redirects should represent deliberate equivalence/continuity decisions, not hide unresolved source differences.

=== Revision / history / diff ===
Native provenance and correction trail. Do not recreate this data in semantic fields without a concrete reason.

== Independent dimensions ==

<pre>
namespace
≠ entity type
≠ domain
≠ Book Matter
≠ epistemic standing
≠ lifecycle
≠ relationship
≠ navigation view
</pre>

This separation is the central V2 organizational correction.

== Ontology ==

[[BITwiki:Ontology]] defines what kinds of objects and relations the knowledge graph represents.

[[BITwiki:Entity types]] contains the current evolving base vocabulary. Types are stable recognition patterns, not rigid bins.

[[BITwiki:Relationships]] defines semantic edges conservatively. Specific relation semantics should not be inferred from mere proximity or category membership.

[[BITwiki:Semantic properties]] documents what V2 actually implements in the Property namespace.

== Domains ==

Domain answers: '''what field/body of knowledge does this belong to?'''

The current top-level browse surface includes:

* Systems science
* Science
* Biology
* Mathematics
* Philosophy
* Technology
* Electronics
* Energy
* Engineering
* Chemistry
* Physics
* Medicine

These are initial high-level knowledge surfaces, not a claim that all knowledge fits into twelve exclusive branches.

A page may span domains. Domain membership is semantic/browse context, not entity identity.

== Book Matter ==

[[BITwiki:Book Matter]] is the reusable anatomy of knowledge.

It allows a coherent parent page to expose meaningful portions such as:

* Overview / Definition;
* Concepts / Frameworks;
* Mechanism / Methods;
* Evidence;
* Applications / Case studies;
* Debate / controversy;
* Timeline / historical development;
* References / bibliography;
* specialized knowledge maps.

Split content only when the part has independent reuse/retrieval value.

'''Modularity does not mean fragmentation.'''

== Epistemics ==

[[BITwiki:Epistemics]] separates:

* epistemic standing;
* evidence;
* source type/evaluation;
* provenance;
* confidence rationale;
* uncertainty;
* review/verification;
* lifecycle;
* perspective.

This prevents a single status field or numerical score from standing in for the full question of how something is known.

== Navigation ==

Navigation is a set of views over the knowledge substrate.

=== Portals ===
High-level entry points into domains or coherent knowledge surfaces. A portal should help a reader enter and traverse a field; it is not an entity superclass.

=== Categories ===
Browse/index groupings. Use when a collection has human navigational value.

=== Indices ===
Explicit curated or generated lists organized for lookup.

=== Outlines ===
Hierarchical views of a body of knowledge useful for orientation and progressive study.

=== Contents pages ===
Reader-oriented maps into a body of material, especially where ordering matters.

=== Semantic queries ===
Dynamic views derived from properties/relations.

=== Knowledge graphs ===
Relationship-oriented views. A graph visualizes ontology; it does not define ontology simply by drawing edges.

== Progressive disclosure ==

V1 Book Matter research correctly treated progressive disclosure as a core capability:

* simple orientation first;
* intermediate structure when needed;
* advanced technical detail available without overwhelming entry-level reading;
* agents retrieve the smallest useful semantic unit for the task.

This principle should shape pages, portals, transclusion, and query output.

== V1 lessons carried forward ==

=== Keep ===

* portals as intelligent entry points;
* modular/transcludable content;
* semantic relationships;
* knowledge-graph navigation;
* polyhierarchy/faceting where useful;
* progressive disclosure;
* explicit evidence/provenance;
* patterns that emerge from use;
* revisions/redirects preserving continuity.

=== Refactor ===

* category-as-filesystem;
* Topic/domain/entity-type conflation;
* one giant taxonomy carrying subject + type + epistemics + workflow;
* numeric confidence as mandatory decoration;
* documentation that describes unimplemented namespace objects as live capability;
* duplicate <code>.REVISED</code>/<code>final</code> publication surfaces;
* arbitrary fragment pages with no independent reuse value.

== Placement test ==

When adding material, ask:

{| class="wikitable"
! Need !! Place / primitive
|-
| ordinary knowledge about a subject || Main page
|-
| project/about-wiki architecture or governance || BITwiki namespace
|-
| reusable content anatomy || Book Matter / transclusion
|-
| repeated rendering/interface pattern || Template
|-
| typed semantic fact/relation || Property
|-
| human browse collection || Category
|-
| high-level knowledge entry point || Portal
|-
| historical V1 source evidence || archive-v1, not deployed V2
|}

